<exercise xml:id="exam1-f24-q11">
<statement>
The graph of a twice-differentiable function
<m>
    f
</m>
is shown in <xref ref="F24Ex1q11"/>.  Which of the following is true?
<figure xml:id="F24Ex1q11">
  <caption> The graph of twice-differentiable <m>f</m>.</caption> 
  <image source="images/GraphOffdoubleprimeFall2024Exam1.jpg" width="50%">
        <description>Graph of a curve increasing from below the x-axis, crossing the x-axis at the filled point (1, 0), then continuing upward and leveling off as x increases.
</description>
      </image>
</figure>
</statement>
<choices randomize="yes">

    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        <p><m>
            f''(1)&lt;f(1)&lt;f'(1)
        </m></p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          Correct.  The concavity is negative; the value is zero, and the slope is positive.  
          </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <choice>
      <statement>
        <p><m>
            f(1)&lt;f'(1)&lt;f''(1)
        </m> </p>
      </statement>
    </choice>

    <choice>
      <statement>
        <p><m>
            f'(1)&lt;f(1)&lt;f''(1)
        </m></p>
      </statement>
    </choice>

     <choice>
      <statement>
        <p><m>
            f(1)&lt;f''(1)&lt;f'(1)
        </m> </p>
      </statement>
    </choice>

    <choice>
      <statement>
        <p><m>
            f''(1)&lt;f'(1)&lt;f(1)
        </m></p>
      </statement>
    </choice>

  </choices>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exam1-f24-q12">
<statement>
<p>

Let <m>f</m> be a function that is <em>even</em> and continuous on the closed interval <m>[-3,3]</m>. The function <m>f</m> and its derivatives
have the properties indicated in the table.
</p>

<tabular halign="center" bottom="minor" top="minor">
<row header="yes">
<cell><m>x</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>0&lt;x&lt;1</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>1&lt;x&lt;2</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>2&lt;x&lt;3</m></cell>
</row>

<row>
<cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell>Positive</cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell>Negative</cell>
<cell><m>-1</m></cell>
<cell>Negative</cell>
</row>

<row>
<cell><m>f'(x)</m></cell>
<cell>Undefined</cell>
<cell>Negative</cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell>Negative</cell>
<cell>Undefined</cell>
<cell>Positive</cell>
</row>

<row>
<cell><m>f''(x)</m></cell>
<cell>Undefined</cell>
<cell>Positive</cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell>Negative</cell>
<cell>Undefined</cell>
<cell>Negative</cell>
</row>
</tabular>

<p>
In the <m>xy</m>-plane provided below, sketch the graph of a function with all the given characteristics of <m>f</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="empty_axis_fall2025_ex1">
  <caption>
Graph the function with given characteristics here.  
  </caption>
  <image xml:id="emptyaxisfall2026ex1" width="50%">
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[thick]
\draw [black!80,line width=0.5pt,-latex] (0,0) -- (4,0) node [below] at (3.75,0) {$x$};
\draw [black!80,line width=0.5pt,-latex] (0,0) -- (-4,0);
\draw [black!80,line width=0.5pt,-latex] (0,0) -- (0,3.5) node [right] at (0,3.25) {$y$};
\draw [black!80,line width=0.5pt,-latex] (0,0) -- (0,-3.5);
\foreach \x/\xtext in {-3/-3,-2/-2,-1/-1,1/1,2/2,3/3}
\draw[shift={(\x,0)}] (0pt,5pt)--(0pt,-5pt) node[below] {$\xtext$};
\foreach \y/\ytext in {-3/-3,-2/-2,-1/-1,1/1,2/2,3/3}
\draw[shift={(0,\y)}] (4pt,0pt)--(-4pt,0pt) node[left] {$\ytext$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>


</statement>

<answer>
  <p>
    Since <m>f</m> is even, the graph is symmetric about the
    <m>y</m>-axis. Therefore, the information given for
    <m>x&gt;0</m> determines the behavior of the graph for
    <m>x&lt;0</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Since <m>f(0)=1</m>, the graph passes through the point
    <m>(0,1)</m>. Since <m>f(1)=0</m> and <m>f(2)=-1</m>,
    the graph also passes through the points
    <m>(1,0)</m>, <m>(2,-1)</m>, <m>(-1,0)</m>, and
    <m>(-2,-1)</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    On the interval <m>(0,1)</m>, we have
    <m>f'(x)&lt;0</m> and <m>f''(x)&gt;0</m>. Therefore,
    the function is decreasing and concave up.
  </p>

  <p>
    At <m>x=1</m>, we have <m>f'(1)=0</m> and
    <m>f''(1)=0</m>. Hence the graph has a horizontal tangent
    line at the point <m>(1,0)</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    On the interval <m>(1,2)</m>, we have
    <m>f'(x)&lt;0</m> and <m>f''(x)&lt;0</m>. Therefore,
    the function is decreasing and concave down.
  </p>

  <p>
    At <m>x=2</m>, the derivatives are undefined, so the graph
    may have a corner, cusp, or other point where differentiability
    fails. The graph passes through <m>(2,-1)</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    On the interval <m>(2,3)</m>, we have
    <m>f'(x)&gt;0</m> and <m>f''(x)&lt;0</m>. Therefore,
    the function is increasing and concave down.
  </p>

  <p>
    Using symmetry, the graph on <m>[-3,0]</m> is the reflection of
    the graph on <m>[0,3]</m> across the <m>y</m>-axis.
  </p>

  <p>
    A correct sketch must satisfy all of the following:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><p>The graph is symmetric about the <m>y</m>-axis.</p></li>
    <li><p>The graph passes through <m>(0,1)</m>, <m>(\pm1,0)</m>, and <m>(\pm2,-1)</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>The graph is decreasing and concave up on <m>(0,1)</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>The graph has a horizontal tangent at <m>x=\pm1</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>The graph is decreasing and concave down on <m>(1,2)</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>The graph is increasing and concave down on <m>(2,3)</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>The graph is not differentiable at <m>x=0</m> and <m>x=\pm2</m>.</p></li>
</ul>

</answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>R(t)</m> represent the rate at which a volume of water
      (in liters) is leaking out of a tank, where <m>t</m> is measured
      in hours after midnight.
    </p>

    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li>
        <p>
          Interpret the meaning of <m>R'(2)=0.25</m>. Be sure to include
          units in your response.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          If the hole in this tank is slowly being closed between time
          <m>t=0</m> and <m>t=5</m> hours, would you say that
          <m>R''(2)</m> is positive, negative, or zero? Explain.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <answer>
    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li>
        <p>
          The statement <m>R'(2)=0.25</m> means that 2 hours after
          midnight, the leakage rate is 
          <m>0.25</m> liters per hour. 
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Since the hole is being closed, the leakage rate should be
          decreasing over time. Therefore <m>R'(t)</m> is decreasing, which means the graph of <m>R</m> is concave down.  Thus,
        </p>

        <md>
          R''(2) &lt; 0.
        </md>

      </li>
    </ol>
  </answer>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      If it exists, determine the value of
    </p>

    <md>
      \lim_{x \to 1}
      \left(
      \frac{1-x}{1-\sqrt{x}}
      \right)^2.
    </md>

    <p>
      If the limit does not exist, explain why it does not.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <answer>
    <p>
      Observe that
    </p>

    <md>
      1-x
      =
      (1-\sqrt{x})(1+\sqrt{x}).
    </md>

    <p>
      So for <m>x \ne 1</m>,
    </p>

    <md>
      \left(
      \frac{1-x}{1-\sqrt{x}}
      \right)^2
      =
      \left(
      \frac{(1-\sqrt{x})(1+\sqrt{x})}
           {1-\sqrt{x}}
      \right)^2
      =
      (1+\sqrt{x})^2.
    </md>

    <p>
      Hence
    </p>

    <md>
      \lim_{x\to 1}
      \left(
      \frac{1-x}{1-\sqrt{x}}
      \right)^2
      =
      \lim_{x\to 1}
      (1+\sqrt{x})^2
      =
      (1+1)^2
      =
      4.
    </md>

    <p>
      A table of values would also provide some reasonable justification for this conclusion.  
    </p>

  </answer>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      <em>Copilot</em> was given the following prompt.
    </p>

<figure xml:id="F24Ex1q15">
  <caption> A graph produced by <em>Copilot</em>.</caption> 
  <image source="images/CopilotPromptContinuousNotDifferentiable12.jpg" width="50%">
        <description>Screenshot of a chat message containing the prompt: “Produce a graph of a function that is continuous at x = 1 and x = 3 but not differentiable at those values.”
</description>
      </image>
</figure>

    <p>
      <em>Copilot</em> returned the graph in <xref ref="F24Ex1q15"/> and gave the
      piecewise-defined function
    </p>

    <md>
f(x)=
\begin{cases}
x^2 &amp; \text{for } x&lt;1,\\
2x-1 &amp; \text{for } 1\le x&lt;3,\\
(x-3)^2+5 &amp; \text{for } x\ge 3.
\end{cases}
    </md>

    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li>
        <p>
          Using a limit, explain how you can be certain that
          <m>f(x)</m> is indeed continuous at <m>x=1</m>.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          We now wish to decide if <em>Copilot</em> is correct about
          <m>f(x)</m> not being differentiable at <m>x=1</m>. To
          investigate, compute the value of
        </p>

        <me>
\lim_{h\to 0^-}
\frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h}.
        </me>

        <p>
          Show all steps in your calculation.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          It can be shown that
        </p>

        <me>
\lim_{h\to 0^+}
\frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h}=2.
        </me>

        <p>
          Using your answer to part (b), determine if <m>f(x)</m> is
          differentiable at <m>x=1</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

</exercise>

<answer>
  <p>
    <ol marker="(a)">
<li>
  <p>
    <md> \lim_{x \rightarrow 1^-} f(x) = \lim_{x \rightarrow 1^-} x^2 = 1</md> and <md> \lim_{x \rightarrow 1+} f(x) = \lim_{x \rightarrow 1^+} 2x-1 = 1 </md> so that <md> \lim_{x \rightarrow 1} f(x) = 1 = f(1).</md>
  </p>
</li>
<li>
  <p>
    <md> <mrow> \lim_{h \rightarrow 0^-} \frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h} \mathstrut \amp = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0^-} \frac{(1+h)^2-(2(1)-1)}{h} </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0^-} \frac{1+2h+h^2-1}{h} </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0^-} \frac{h(2+h)}{h} </mrow> 
      <mrow> \amp = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0^-} 2+h </mrow> 
      <mrow> \amp = 2</mrow></md>
  </p>
</li>
<li>
  <p>
    Yes.  <m> f(x) </m> is differentiable at <m> x=1</m> since <m>\lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h} = 2</m> implies <m>f'(1)=2</m>.
  </p>
</li>
    </ol>
  </p>
</answer>

</exercises>

</section>
</handout>